{%- comment -%}
  ── THE DELIVERY ANSWER, BEFORE ANYONE HAS TO ASK ────────────────────────────
  Renders on every product page, under the buy button.

  WHY IT EXISTS
  The product page described condition and returns and said nothing at all
  about delivery. Meanwhile the two most common questions arriving as DMs on
  Instagram and Facebook were "do you deliver?" and "how do I order?". A
  shopper who has to open a chat to find out whether a parcel can reach them
  is a shopper who mostly does not bother.

  WHY THE NUMBERS ARE HARD-CODED HERE AND NOT PULLED FROM A SETTING
  There is no Liquid object that exposes a shipping RATE — `shop.shipping_policy`
  is prose written by hand, and rates live in Settings → Shipping where the
  storefront cannot read them. So these two figures are the one thing in this
  file that can drift out of step with checkout.

  They are not guesses. They were read off 41 real orders placed since the
  28 August 2026 rate change (the day "Shipping R130" became "Standard
  delivery"): every order with a subtotal at or above R1,000 shipped free
  (19 of them, R1,000–R9,700) and every order below it was charged R99
  (22 of them, R250–R850). No order contradicted the rule.

  IF THE RATES CHANGE, CHANGE THEM HERE. Nothing else reads them.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- assign free_from = 1000 -%}
{%- assign flat_rate = 99 -%}

<div class="delivery-promise" role="note">
  <p class="delivery-promise__line">
    <strong>Delivered anywhere in South Africa.</strong>
  </p>
  <p class="delivery-promise__line">
    R{{ flat_rate }} delivery — free on orders over R{{ free_from }}.
  </p>
  {%- if shop.shipping_policy.body != blank -%}
    <p class="delivery-promise__line delivery-promise__link">
      <a href="{{ shop.shipping_policy.url }}">Delivery details</a>
    </p>
  {%- endif -%}
</div>

{%- comment -%}
  Styles are inline-scoped to the snippet so this cannot be broken by, or
  break, the theme's global stylesheet. Colours are inherited rather than
  stated, so the block follows the page in both the light and dark treatments
  rather than pinning a background that only suits one of them.
{%- endcomment -%}
<style>
  .delivery-promise {
    margin: 1.2rem 0 0;
    padding: 1.1rem 1.2rem;
    border: 1px solid rgba(var(--color-foreground, 18 18 18), 0.18);
    border-radius: 0.6rem;
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .delivery-promise__line {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .delivery-promise__line + .delivery-promise__line {
    margin-top: 0.35rem;
  }
  .delivery-promise__link a {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
</style>
